WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.228 --> 00:00:03.076
(light piano music)

00:00:03.076 --> 00:00:03.971
- People need to understand

00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:06.976
that all of North America
is indigenous land.

00:00:06.976 --> 00:00:08.058
It is Native land.

00:00:08.058 --> 00:00:10.056
(light piano music)

00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:12.540
People tend to forget that there was

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.218
a vibrant Native culture

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:17.393
living in all parts of North America.

00:00:17.393 --> 00:00:19.224
And food is really the piece

00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:21.148
that's going to help us move forward

00:00:21.148 --> 00:00:22.645
into a brighter future.

00:00:22.645 --> 00:00:25.645
(light piano music)

00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:32.131
My name is Sean Sherman

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:33.464
and I am a chef.

00:00:34.474 --> 00:00:36.671
I own the company The Sioux Chef.

00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:40.440
So this is gonna be two
pieces per plate for these.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.458
Our mission is revitalizing
Native American foods

00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:47.958
and re-identifying North American cuisine.

00:00:50.386 --> 00:00:53.454
We've tried really hard
to maintain authenticity

00:00:53.454 --> 00:00:57.184
by removing all of the
colonial ingredients

00:00:57.184 --> 00:00:59.901
like dairy, wheat flour, processed sugar

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.497
beef, pork, chicken

00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:03.074
and we're just using a lot of wild game

00:01:03.074 --> 00:01:04.620
a lot of the native agricultural

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.759
heirloom varietals, a lot
of the wild foods around us

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:09.460
and really making those plates

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.543
that taste like a region.

00:01:13.541 --> 00:01:16.636
Most of these ingredients
are very familiar to people

00:01:16.636 --> 00:01:19.560
but the usage of a lot of the wild plants

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.977
really talk about a place in a community.

00:01:26.344 --> 00:01:29.089
We prioritize purchasing from
the indigenous vendors first

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:31.419
because we really want to
open up a lot of opportunity

00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:34.081
for indigenous people growing out farms

00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:35.277
or collecting things from the wild

00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:36.678
or raising animals.

00:01:36.678 --> 00:01:37.735
So for the big dinner

00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:40.165
we're actually cooking this bison down

00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:42.817
with a bunch of fresh
cedar and some bergamot.

00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:44.677
We will purchase from anybody

00:01:44.677 --> 00:01:46.395
raising these indigenous pieces

00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:48.277
because it is such an important part

00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:50.544
of the landscape and the history.

00:01:50.544 --> 00:01:52.127
I know you want it.

00:01:54.055 --> 00:01:55.096
As we travel around the country

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:56.580
we like to make dinners and foods

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.413
that really represent those areas.

00:02:00.622 --> 00:02:02.492
What's great about being
able to do these dinners

00:02:02.492 --> 00:02:05.926
especially in non-indigenous communities

00:02:05.926 --> 00:02:07.848
is just to bring awareness.

00:02:07.848 --> 00:02:09.109
This kind of food that we're eating today

00:02:09.109 --> 00:02:11.154
like, you're gonna feel really
good when you leave here.

00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:13.598
You're not gonna feel
heavy and weighted down.

00:02:13.598 --> 00:02:16.792
You're gonna feel energized and just happy

00:02:16.792 --> 00:02:20.098
because you're knowing where
this food is coming from.

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:23.228
We feel like anybody across North America

00:02:23.228 --> 00:02:25.506
will benefit from the understanding

00:02:25.506 --> 00:02:28.064
of the indigenous food systems around you.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:29.742
And it's all gonna help us move forward.

00:02:29.742 --> 00:02:32.575
(uplifting music)

